WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.835
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We are here with Jeremy Irons.

00:00:02.835 --> 00:00:04.837
Now, I have read
that you actually,

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:07.707
in your youth, considered a life
in the military.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:08.908
Is this true?

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.442
-No, I never considered it.

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:12.078
It was considered for me.

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:15.515
When I -- When I left school,
I had very few --

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.050
I wasn't very intellectual.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:20.286
I had very few grades, you know,
couldn't go to university,

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:22.521
and they thought --
I had done very well at school.

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:27.026
We had a sort of cadet corps,
and I got to the top.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:29.696
I was, can you believe,
a regimental sergeant major,

00:00:29.696 --> 00:00:30.997
which was the real gig.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.999
Loads of stuff hanging off me,

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.501
and epaulets and sashes
and things.

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:38.471
And I was extremely fierce,
and so they thought,

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.473
"He'll go into
the paratroopers or something,

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:41.774
and fall out of airplanes."

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:44.911
And...
But I didn't want to.

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:46.679
What they hadn't realized
was the only reason

00:00:46.679 --> 00:00:50.649
I did this cadet training
was it got me away from school.

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:52.551
It got me out in the mountains.

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.821
In fact, there was one time
when they --

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.825
I'd left, 'cause you could leave
when you were 16 or something.

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:02.195
And I was 16 1/2, and they said,

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:03.896
"Listen, would you come back
to this camp?

00:01:03.896 --> 00:01:06.399
Will you run this summer camp
in Brecon Beacons,"

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:08.100
which are mountains in Wales.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.837
And I said, "Oh, God."
Well, I said, "I'll do a deal.

00:01:11.837 --> 00:01:16.976
If you give me a Jeep and
a section of about seven men,

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:21.281
and let me
be a sort of terrorist

00:01:21.281 --> 00:01:23.482
for all your great, great
operations" --

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:26.686
where thousands of boys
have to sort of

00:01:26.686 --> 00:01:29.288
walk on their stomachs
through brush

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:30.523
and in the middle
of the night --

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:34.060
It's horrible, and with rifles
and things --

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:36.629
I said, "If you let me
break up that

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.064
and capture people

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.800
and take them off in the Jeep,
I'll do that."

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.234
And we had
a most wonderful week.

00:01:42.234 --> 00:01:43.703
Wonderful week.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.039
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:48.140
But it was just really
to get away from school.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.341
That was why I went.

00:01:49.341 --> 00:01:52.044
-That is one of the most
loveliest,

00:01:52.044 --> 00:01:53.880
sadistic stories
I've ever heard.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.315
-Yeah, yeah, well --
-So you also --

00:01:56.315 --> 00:02:01.754
you also looked at
a life in the circus.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.124
-I wanted --
I wanted to be a gypsy.

00:02:04.124 --> 00:02:05.992
I didn't want to spend
any more time

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:08.127
with the sort of people
I'd been educated with,

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:11.264
who were all going on to be
bankers or military

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:12.765
or businesspeople,

00:02:12.765 --> 00:02:14.334
and I thought,
"I can't bear these people."

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.403
And so, "I want to be a gypsy.
I want to travel."

00:02:17.403 --> 00:02:20.673
And I played guitar, and I used
to busk in London's West End

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:24.143
and earn my money that way.

00:02:24.143 --> 00:02:27.546
And I thought,
"The circus or the carnival,

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.849
maybe that would be great.

00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:32.251
I can travel around with this
group of colorful people

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:35.321
and ride horses or be
a trapeze artist or something."

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:39.491
And so then I went and I looked
at the accommodation.

00:02:39.491 --> 00:02:42.227
I went to a circus,
and I went to a carnival

00:02:42.227 --> 00:02:44.130
and looked at where they lived.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.567
And they live in
these sort of caravans

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:49.402
which had doors at the side,

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:54.707
and you go up these steps and
go in and there's four people.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.243
One, two, three, four.
That's where they live.

00:02:57.243 --> 00:02:59.945
I said, "Well, I'm too
middle-class for this.

00:02:59.945 --> 00:03:03.315
I -- I need a house.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:05.018
I need a mortgage, I think."
[ Laughter ]

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:06.886
So then I looked at the theatre,

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:10.523
and I got a job in a theatre
and saw how I liked that.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:12.825
And it was sort of similar,
in a way,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.528
in that you worked odd hours.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.196
You know, you worked
in the evening.

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:19.832
But I love the people,
I love the smells,

00:03:19.832 --> 00:03:23.870
and so then I thought
I better go and learn to do it.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.938
So then I went off
to theatre school.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:28.140
-When they showed you
the housing at the circus,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:31.810
did you say, "I was hoping for
something a little bit more --

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:34.047
just a little bit more like --"
-Yeah, that's right.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:37.717
-You mentioned busking and
playing guitar in the West End.

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.685
So, you know,
that's a way to make money,

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:40.786
but what were you doing?

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:42.221
You were just sort of
walking around,

00:03:42.221 --> 00:03:44.356
or were you in one location,
did you move around?

00:03:44.356 --> 00:03:46.025
-No, it was very hard

00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:49.028
because the locations
are greatly valued.

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:50.696
You have to get there early,

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:52.931
and then people come
and kick you off sometimes.

00:03:52.931 --> 00:03:55.234
But it's cinema queues,
basically, I would do.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:59.304
Cinema queue, you could make
sort of 5 pounds, 6 pounds.

00:03:59.304 --> 00:04:03.910
And in those days I was earning
2 pounds, 10 a week.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:08.014
So, to earn 5 pounds in
half an hour was like nirvana,

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:10.282
and it was, of course,
also a wonderful way

00:04:10.282 --> 00:04:11.917
to pick up girls.
-Oh, perfect.

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.686
-Meet girls anyway.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:17.089
-I haven't gotten
a chance to speak to you since

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.792
sort of the show of a couple
years ago, "Watchmen," finished.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.861
-Right.
-And it was such a wonderful

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.864
show, and I'm wondering,
you know,

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.299
for someone like you,
who maybe didn't know

00:04:27.299 --> 00:04:29.335
the comic book,
did you understand

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:31.003
what you were getting into
when you took on that role?

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.704
-I didn't have a clue.

00:04:32.704 --> 00:04:35.908
I had no clue, from beginning
to end, what I was doing.

00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:39.511
I met our writer,
our showrunner, fantastic man.

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:43.516
We had a lunch in Los Angeles,
and he talked for 2 hours,

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:46.219
and I understood nothing
of what he was saying.

00:04:46.219 --> 00:04:47.954
[ Laughter ]
But what I saw

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:50.756
was huge enthusiasm
and huge intelligence

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.124
and huge imagination.

00:04:52.124 --> 00:04:53.759
I thought, "Well, I don't know
what we're going to do,

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:55.695
but this is going to be
really interesting."

00:04:55.695 --> 00:04:58.464
It was not going to be boring.
I could tell that.

00:04:58.464 --> 00:04:59.866
So I said, "Yes, I'll do it."

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:02.802
But while we were shooting,
I had not a clue.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:04.002
Actually, when I watched it,

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:05.671
I didn't understand much of it,
either.

00:05:05.671 --> 00:05:07.139
[ Laughter ]

00:05:07.139 --> 00:05:10.276
-As someone who understood it, I
just thought you were fantastic.

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:12.077
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, good.

00:05:12.077 --> 00:05:13.579
-Hey, thank you for being here,
Jeremy.

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:15.514
It's always such a pleasure.
Next time, in person.

00:05:15.514 --> 00:05:17.616
-Yes, sir. Take care.

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.084
Give my love to Mike.

00:05:19.084 --> 00:05:20.919
-Jeremy Irons, everybody.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:23.188
"Munich: The Edge of War"
is in theaters now

00:05:23.188 --> 00:05:25.558
and available on Netflix
January 21st.

